<tr><th class="News">11-11</th><td class="News"><a title="Arctic Linux is a Fedora-based desktop Linux distribution featuring the keyboard-friendly Mango tiling window manager. It uses the Quickshell desktop shell. The distribution can be tested in live mode, but it can also be installed to a hard disk using a custom graphical system installation wizard - either from the initial boot menu or by launching the Arctic installer while in live mode. The installer, which employs disk encryption on a Btrfs filesystem, can download and install additional software. Arctic Linux provides its own repository of custom applications, while also supporting installation of flatpak packages and integration of Nix packages from NixOS." href="arctic">Arctic Linux</a></td></tr>
<tr><th class="News">11-10</th><td class="News"><a title="VoidStation is a media center Linux distribution based on Void. It features the Openbox window manager customised to present available applications as large tiles, so that they can be viewed easily and launched from a distance by a remote device, such as gamepads, remote controls, mice, keyboard, game console controllers, USB pads and joysticks. The user interface is configured to open an on-screen keyboard for every input field. Additional applications, games, gaming platforms and streaming services can be added using the included AppCenter. VoidStation also comes with pre-configured themes, adjustable scaling and resolution options, and support for all major European languages." href="voidstation">VoidStation</a></td></tr>
<tr><th class="News">11-09</th><td class="News"><a title="VeltOS is a Debian-based desktop Linux distribution that features a KDE Plasma desktop customised to resemble the default workspace of Microsoft Windows 11. It is designed for users who wish to switch from Windows to Linux, but prefer a desktop with a familiar layout and workflow. The distribution comes with several local artificial intelligence models, includes the Wine compatibility layer for running Windows software, and provides out-of-the-box support for English and Turkish languages." href="veltos">VeltOS</a></td></tr>
<tr><th class="News">11-08</th><td class="News"><a title="MassOS is an independently-developed, lightweight Linux distribution for laptops and desktops. It is distributed in the form of a live ISO image featuring a customised Xfce desktop and graphical system installer or, alternatively, as a rootfs tarball. The distribution does not include any package management utility, but additional software can be installed via third-party tools, such as flatpak, AppImage or Homebrew. The installed system can be upgraded using either the &quot;Repair&quot; option in the boot menu of a newly released ISO image, or by downloading and running the &quot;massos-upgrade&quot; tool. MassOS is designed to be easy and intuitive for persons new to Linux, while also providing flexibility and configurability for more experienced Linux users." href="massos">MassOS</a></td></tr>
<tr><th class="News">11-07</th><td class="News"><a title="BcalcOS is a Devuan-based desktop Linux distribution featuring a themed Xfce desktop. It uses the XLibre display server (rather than X.Org Server or Wayland) and it ships with Brave Origin as the default web browser. The distribution provides its own graphical system installer, a custom-built welcome center and a repository of additional packages that are not available upstream. BcalcOS is designed for users who value a minimalist and simple systemd-free Linux distribution." href="bcalcos">BcalcOS</a></td></tr>
<tr><th class="News">11-04</th><td class="News"><a title="PH4NTXM is a Debian-based live Linux distribution that features a customised Xfce desktop and an extensive range of privacy tools that mask the user's identity. Some of the tools include adaptive identity engine with a linked identity for each session, profile-aligned hardware and system reports, packet transformation and firewall protection, choice of identity modes (&quot;Linux&quot; or &quot;Windows&quot; with encrypted DNS and Firefox browser, &quot;Lone Wolf&quot; with Tor DNS and Tor Browser), disposable live sessions with no automatic persistence, user-defined session password for screen unlock and administration, health diagnostics, offline sandboxed media viewers, and many others. The distribution is available as two separate ISO images with light (&quot;Ghost&quot;) or dark (&quot;Abyss&quot;) desktop interfaces." href="ph4ntxm">PH4NTXM</a></td></tr>
<tr><th class="News">11-03</th><td class="News"><a title="EnderOS is a live, Arch-based Linux distribution featuring the Xfce desktop. It uses a custom-built, real-time Linux kernel. The distribution is meant to be used in live mode; it is designed for users who need a portable, disposable and privacy-respecting operating system, or for those who require a latency-free kernel for their work. The EnderOS live image ships with a range of productivity and multimedia applications, such as LibreOffice office suite, GIMP image editor, Kdenlive video editor and Parole media player, as well as some software development tools and code editors." href="enderos">EnderOS</a></td></tr>
<tr><th class="News">11-02</th><td class="News"><a title="Cutefish is a desktop Linux distribution based on Debian's stable branch and featuring a custom Wayland compositor that bears the same name. It was originally launched in 2021 as CutefishOS, but its development was later abandoned before it was relaunched in November 2026 under its current name. The Cutefish compositor provides a desktop layout similar to the one used by MacOS and it ships with several native applications built with Cutefish Toolkit, including Files, Terminal, Settings, Calendar, System Monitor, Text Editor, Video Player and Screenshot. Cutefish is distributed as a live ISO image for both x86_64 and AArch64 computers, but it's also available as a DEB package compatible with the latest Debian stable release." href="cutefish">Cutefish</a></td></tr>
<tr><th class="News">10-27</th><td class="News"><a title="Gnomarchy is a rolling-release, developer-friendly Linux distribution based on Arch Linux, with components borrowed from Omarchy. It features a customised, keyboard-friendly GNOME desktop. The distribution comes with various features, such as a custom Command Center, a unified cross-desktop theme engine, system-wide artificial intelligence dictation, context file actions in the Nautilus file manager, desktop reminders and alarms, Btrfs and Snapper rollbacks, Ubuntu-style left dock, single-command gaming stack installation, Flatpak and Bazaar app store, Brave Origin as the default browser, pre-installed Telegram and Discord clients, among many others. Gnomarchy also includes a custom command-line application to control various aspects of the desktop, including themes, tiling, snapshots, keymaps, web apps, the gaming stack and the update path." href="gnomarchy">Gnomarchy</a></td></tr>
<tr><th class="News">10-26</th><td class="News"><a title="hybrid-d77 is a proof-of-concept distribution based on Chimera Linux, a project that combines the Linux kernel with the BSD userland (FreeBSD's chimerautils) and the APK package manager from Alpine Linux. Hence the reason for the word &quot;hybrid&quot; in the distribution's name - it refers to the unusual fusion of various BSD and Linux components the result of which is a unique, hybrid operating system. The project offers two separate live images that feature either the niri compositor with the custom-built Utumno desktop shell (a Quickshell fork) or the Sway compositor with a Waybar bar. hybrid-d77 uses the Dinit init system and provides two system installation programs, a command-line one as well as a graphical installation tool called &quot;Mocinha&quot;." href="hybridd77">hybrid-d77</a></td></tr>
<tr><th class="News">10-25</th><td class="News"><a title="Ryoku is an Arch-based Linux distribution designed for desktop use, with optional gaming and local artificial intelligence (AI) extensions. It boots into a text-mode system installer that guides the user through the installation process. Among the available desktop choices are three Wayland compositors (the default Hyprland, as well as niri and Wayfire), all of which use the Quickshell desktop shell. The distribution provides a comprehensive set of desktop configuration tools for configuring workspaces, Quickshell top bar, launcher, feature sidebar and lock screen. A custom agent called Rashin, a machine-generated knowledge vault that collects information about the computer's hardware, desktop settings, packages and user changes, is also available." href="ryoku">Ryoku</a></td></tr>
