        <td class="NewsText" style="font-size: 15px">Tomáš Matějíček has announced the release of <a href="slax">Slax</a> 2026.11, a major new update of the project's minimalist, Debian-based live Linux distribution with a customised Fluxbox window manager. This new version comes with a refreshed desktop look and a built-int AI assistant: "<span class="Quote">Slax 2026.11 is out. For a while I honestly thought there would never be another Slax release. And yet, here we go again. I wanted a Linux desktop I could take anywhere and actually enjoy using. This is the one I wanted to make. The desktop got a proper rebuild. Hold Alt and tap Tab and you can see the windows you're switching between. Hover over a taskbar icon and you get live previews of all the windows from that app. Click the one you want. Alt+F3 shows all four workspaces at once, so you can find that window you were absolutely sure you’d left right here. You can keep files and folders on the desktop, switch keyboard layouts from the taskbar, and adjust the volume by scrolling over the speaker. It finally looks like one desktop. Linux apps often look like they arrived from different decades, sometimes in the same afternoon. In Slax, apps share a consistent look. Open the file manager, an older utility and a newer app side by side. They look like they belong on the same computer. I'm ridiculously pleased about this.</span>" See the <a href="https://www.slax.org/blog/49064-Slax-2026-11-is-out.html">release announcement</a> and the <a href="https://www.slax.org/changelog.php">changelog</a> for all the details and screenshots. <a href="https://ftp.fi.muni.cz/pub/linux/slax/Slax-2026/">Download</a>: <a href="https://slax.org/download/slax-2026.11-64bit.iso">slax-2026.11-64bit.iso</a> (512MB, <a href="table.php?distribution=slax&amp;pkglist=true&amp;version=2026.11#pkglist">pkglist</a>).</td>
